    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-4436",
      "problem": "If\n${A} = \n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n        2 & -1 \\\n        -3 & k\n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack$\nand\n$\\det({A}) = 5$, what is the value of $k?$",
      "worked_solution": "For a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14272902/assets/f06afc44a984879f62f9d61e7a0c68994cd78eca21084fe670a3fefbb54070d5.svg)\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot k-(-1)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =2k-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det(A)=5,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =5 \\\\ 2k-3 & =5 \\\\ 2k & =8 \\\\ k & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$",
